Let's consider a general equilibrium reaction in which $A_1$ and $A_2$ react to form $A_3$ and $A_4$
\begin{center}\ce{$\underbrace{\textcolor{red}{\ce{$\nu_1$A_1_{(Aq)} + $\nu_2$A_2_{(Aq)}}}}_{\textrm{Reactants}}$ <=> $\underbrace{\textcolor{blue}{\ce{$\nu_3$A_3_{(Aq)} + $\nu_4$A_4_{(Aq)}}}}_{\textrm{Products}}$}
\end{center}
The stoichiometric coefficients of the reaction are $\nu_1$, $\nu_2$, $\nu_3$, and $\nu_4$. Cato Maximilian Gulberg and Peter Waage proposed the \emph{law of mass action} in 1864 to describe a chemical system in equilibrium. The expression for the equilibrium constant, which defines the law of mass action, depends only on the concentration of the reactants and products at fixed temperature. To refer to concentration, we will use square brackets. For example, $\big[ A_1 \big]$ means the concentration of $A_1$. Hence, the expression of the equilibrium constant will be:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{  K_{c}=\frac{\big[\text{Products}\big]}{\big[\text{Reactants}\big]}=\frac{\big[ A_3 \big]^{\nu_3} \cdot \big[ A_4 \big]^{\nu_4}} {\big[ A_1 \big]^{\nu_1} \cdot \big[ A_2 \big]^{\nu_2}} } \quad \textcolor{blue}{\text{Equilibrium constant}K_c}\label{\chapterlabel:equation1}
\end{equation}
%\resizeableyellownote{2.5}{1}{Add  Formula \textcolor{blue}{\ref{formula1:1}} to your flashcard.}
Let us break down the expression of $K_c$. On top of the fraction, we have the equilibrium concentration of the products to the power of its coefficients. For example, $\big[ A_3 \big]^{\nu_3}$ means the equilibrium concentration of $A_3$ to the power of the coefficient $\nu_3$. In the bottom of the fraction, we have the concentrations of the reactants to the power of their coefficients. All concentrations in $K_{c}$ are timed.
