Let's focus on the reaction below:
\begin{center}\ce{C6H_{12}O_6_{(Aq)} + 6O2_{(Aq)} <=> 6CO2_{(Aq)} + 6H2O_{(Aq)}}\end{center}

The glucose burning in the body, also known as cellular respiration. This is a controlled, multi-step biochemical process where glucose and oxygen are converted into carbon dioxide, water, as well as usable energy. The expression of the equilibrium constant for this process would be:
\begin{equation*}
  K_{c}=\frac{\big[ \ce{CO2} \big]^6 \cdot \big[ \ce{H2O} \big]^6} {\big[ \ce{C6H_{12}O_6} \big] \cdot \big[ \ce{O2} \big]^6} 
\end{equation*}
As a final note, the equilibrium constant is a dimensionless property. This is because, in its more general definition, equilibrium constants are defined in terms of \emph{chemical activities}, a general dimensionless property that relates to the amount of substance (for example, it can be concentration for solutions, pressure for gases).